NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000079 "=head1 NAME"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000080 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000081 "Sub::Name - (Re)name a sub"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000082 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000083 "=head1 VERSION"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000084 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000085 "version 0.28"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000086 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000087 "=head1 SYNOPSIS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000088 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000089 "    use Sub::Name;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000090 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000091 "    subname $name, $subref;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000092 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000093 "    $subref = subname foo => sub { ... };"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000094 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000095 "=head1 DESCRIPTION"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000096 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000097 "This module has only one function, which is also exported by default:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000098 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000099 "=for stopwords subname"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000100 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000101 "=head2 subname NAME, CODEREF"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000102 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000103 "Assigns a new name to referenced sub.  If package specification is omitted in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000104 "the name, then the current package is used.  The return value is the sub."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000105 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000106 "The name is only used for informative routines (caller, Carp, etc).  You won't"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000107 "be able to actually invoke the sub by the given name.  To allow that, you need"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000108 "to do glob-assignment yourself."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000109 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000110 "Note that for anonymous closures (subs that reference lexicals declared outside"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000111 "the sub itself) you can name each instance of the closure differently, which"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000112 "can be very useful for debugging."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000113 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000114 "=head1 SEE ALSO"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000115 ""
